			<div class="forum_comment_action_menu" style="display: none;" id="forum_comment_actions_menu_583936001036091278">				<a class="forum_comment_action report tooltip" href="javascript:Forum_ShowReportDialog( '103582791475596239', '807973945275927412', '583935635494110426', '46219132', '583936001036091278' );" data-tooltip-text="Denunciar este mensaje por contenido inapropiado. Los mensajes denunciados son marcados para que sean revisados por los moderadores.">
				<span class="non_breaking_space">&nbsp;</span><img src="https://community.fastly.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/notification_icon_flag_dark.png?v=1">Denunciar este mensaje</a>
			</div>
							</div>
			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_583936001036091278">
				<blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Publicado originalmente por <b><a href="#c583935635494111793">CyberDown</a></b>:</div>Meanwhile real humans unable to recreate the magic in mmorpgs that happened 20-25 years ago.  But its popular to hate AI so people do.  Only chance we have getting a good mmorpg will be due to AI and lack of investors. </blockquote><br>Mmorpgs aren't that different from those years back then. What has changed is the world itself. Mmos used to be what social media is to us today. Internet is normalized, people we interact with are just names instead of actual people behind them. You have people cussing or starting fights over nothing. Socializing with people across the world is normalized, there is no more &quot;magic&quot; in that. Newer generations are super competitive and achievement focused so they rush content like bunch of druggies ignoring online aspect of socialization in mmos.<br><br>We got to enjoy best years of mmorpgs xD it's over old man			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
